 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1787). Words follow the Schirmer vocal score's spelling, punctuation and syllables: quà with its accent throughout; \"lontan non sia di quà, no!\" (the libretto has non fia and no final no); \"uom'\" with an apostrophe on its own note; \"passeggiam\" (libretto passeggian) and \"dobbiam\" (libretto dobbiamo); in bar 15 the print hyphenates fa-re al-la-mor with no apostrophe, so the word is written allamor (IPA al.laˈmor), while in bar 39 all' has its own note before a-mor (all' + amor); in bar 25 the print's \"ad-dos-so un-gran\" is two words, un (sung on one note with -so) and gran; cos' and è are sung on separate notes and written as two words. Don Giovanni, disguised in Leporello's hat and cloak, sings the whole text, then its first two stanzas again with many repeats (ferite; e spada al fianco egli ha five times; fate presto), and the last stanza with e già vedrai cos'è repeated to the end. IPA, word meanings and translations were drafted for this app (S39) and the IPA was checked against Castel. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
